211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 194 0 R
/T (A Message from Mayor Zack Stratton)
/E (A Message from Mayor Zack Stratton)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Dear Vineyard Residents,)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
213 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (As we move into March, I want to thank you for the opportunity to serve as your mayor. Over the past several weeks, the new administration and City Council have been working to carefully review city operations and address issues that directly affect our families and businesses.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
214 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (One issue that came to our attention in late February involved the Transportation Utility Fee \(TUF\). Several local business owners began raising concerns on social media after receiving utility bills that were significantly higher than they had expected. As these concerns were brought forward, our administration quickly began reviewing the issue to better understand what had occurred.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (During that review, we discovered that a Transportation Utility Fee had been approved by the previous City Council during their final month in office last December. As the policy began to take effect, businesses began receiving utility bills reflecting substantial increases. In one instance, a local business reported their monthly bill increasing from $411 to $5,357!)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (While funding transportation infrastructure is important for a growing city like Vineyard, funding policies must also be responsible and take into consideration our economic engine that drives the future success of our community.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (On March 5th, I issued an Executive Order temporarily pausing enforcement actions, penalties, and fines associated with the Transportation Utility Fee. This action allows the city time to conduct a comprehensive review of the program, including the methodology used to calculate the fee, the financial assumptions behind it, and the overall impact it may have on Vineyard residents and local businesses.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
218 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Our goal is to ensure that any long-term solution for transportation funding is fiscally responsible while also being fair and sustainable for the community.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Beyond this issue, our administration continues to focus on improving city services, strengthening communication with residents, and ensuring Vineyard remains a place where families can afford to live and businesses can succeed.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
220 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (This month also brings opportunities for residents to come together through community events, including the Vineyard Gold Rush 5K and Kids 1K at Grove Park, which highlights the strong sense of community that makes Vineyard such a special place to live.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
221 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Vineyard is one of the fastest-growing cities in Utah, and with that growth comes both opportunity and responsibility. Our commitment is to guide that growth thoughtfully while preserving the quality of life that residents expect and deserve.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
222 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Thank you for staying engaged and for being part of the future of Vineyard. It is an honor to serve you.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
